course: world
lesson: 161
title: Lucky socks and Grandma's superstitions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grandad wears his "lucky" red socks for every big match. When his team wins he says, "See? The socks did it." When they lose in the same socks, he finds an excuse: "they were in the wash the day before." He remembers the hits and forgets the misses.
explain: A superstition is a rule with no reason behind it that people keep anyway: black cats, ladders, umbrellas indoors, broken mirrors, the number 13, touching wood.
explain: The big trap is "it happened after, so it was caused by it". Nan drops a jar of jam after a black cat crosses the road, but coming first is not the same as causing. Jars drop when hands are full or slippery.
explain: The fair test is counting: every match with the socks and every match without, every day with a crow cawing and every day without. Counted honestly, the lucky thing makes no difference at all.
explain: Families take the mickey (tease and make fun) out of the reasoning, not the person: "Grandad, the players can't even see your socks, mate." Grandad can love his socks and still laugh along.
fact: A superstition is a belief that an object, action or number brings good or bad luck, with nothing that really connects them.
fact: "It happened after, so it was caused by it" is a trap; grown-ups call it post hoc. Coming first is not the same as causing.
fact: Remembering the times that fit your idea and forgetting the times that don't is called confirmation bias.
fact: A fair test of a lucky charm counts all the cases, with it and without it; tested like that, lucky socks, touching wood and cawing crows make no difference.
fact: A rule that explains every result, good or bad, can never be wrong, so it tells you nothing; inventing a new excuse each time it fails is called moving the goalposts.
fact: Black cats are ordinary cats; in Britain and Japan a black cat is often called good luck, which shows these "rules" are made up and differ from place to place.
fact: The one sensible reason not to walk under a ladder is safety: a tool or a paint pot could fall; stepping into a busy road to avoid it is more dangerous.
fact: Opening an umbrella indoors can knock a lamp or poke an eye, so care is sensible, but it cannot make anything happen a week later.
fact: Renaming a floor does not change its height: in a hotel whose lift buttons go 12, 14, the floor marked 14 is really the 13th floor.
fact: In any week some bad luck happens to everyone, so a "curse" can always find something to blame.
fact: Better marks come from practice, not from mirrors, shoes on tables or the end of a curse.
fact: Words people use: "aye" means yes (Scotland and the North), "didnae" means didn't and "no'" means not (Scotland), "pure" means really (Glasgow), "Ammamma" is grandma in Malayalam (Kerala, India), "Abuela" is grandma in Spanish, and "big brain moment" and "absolute weapon of a plan" are sarcastic ways to call an idea silly.
exercise: Grandad's team wins in his lucky socks; last month they lost in the same socks and he said they had been in the wash. What is wrong with his thinking? | He counts the wins and explains away the losses | He should wash his lucky socks much less often, or never at all | Red socks only work for home matches | He forgot to wear his lucky hat as well
exercise: Nan drops a jar of jam after a black cat crossed the road that morning. What is the broken step? | She thinks the cat caused it because the cat came first | She didn't check whether the cat was really black | She forgot that black cats are good luck in the afternoon | Nothing, cats really do make people clumsy
exercise: Uncle Ray steps into the road to go round a ladder and a cyclist nearly hits him. Which comeback hits his reasoning? | "So the superstition nearly got you flattened, and you're thanking it?" | "Ladders are lucky, mate, you should have gone straight under it instead." | "Cyclists are always bad luck, everybody knows that." | "You should have touched wood before you stepped out."
exercise: What is the one sensible reason not to walk under a ladder? | A tool or paint pot could fall on you | It gives you seven years of bad luck | It upsets the person up the ladder | It breaks a very old rule
exercise: Mum says someone opened an umbrella indoors and "the dog was sick the next week". What is the trap? | Joining any later bad event to the "bad luck" thing | Forgetting that umbrellas only bring good luck when they're opened outdoors | Not opening the umbrella wide enough | Thinking dogs are afraid of umbrellas
exercise: Callum put new shoes on the table and got a bad spelling mark the next day. What really caused the bad mark? | He didn't practise his spelling words | The new shoes on the table | Gran's warning made him nervous on purpose | The teacher saw the shoes and marked him down
exercise: How could Callum fairly test Gran's shoe rule? | Put shoes on the table many times and count good and bad days | Do it just once more, and see whether anything bad happens tomorrow | Ask Gran whether she still believes it | Never do it again, so he can't be wrong
exercise: Priya's aunt touches wood and had a good year. What is missing from her proof? | A comparison with people who never touch wood | A bigger table to touch every day | Touching metal as well as wood | A list of all the days when she forgot to touch any wood
exercise: Liam's dad's team loses 3-0 and he says someone must have touched his unwashed shirt. What is the trap? | Saving a belief with a new excuse each time it fails | Thinking that clean shirts always make a football team play better | Forgetting to watch the whole match | Remembering only the matches they lost
exercise: Abuela says sweeping at night sweeps out luck; Sofia sweeps, finds a pound coin, and Abuela says she "didn't sweep hard enough". Why is the rule useless? | It explains every result, so it can never be wrong | It only works on certain days of the week | It needs a stronger, stiffer broom before it can work properly | It is true, but only for crisps
exercise: The hotel lift goes 12, 14. What is true about the floor marked 14? | It is really the 13th floor up | It is 14 floors up because the button says so | It is safer because floor 13 was left out | It is the same height as floor 12
exercise: Noah's grandma says the crow told her the cousins were coming. How did she really know? | The cousins phoned on Thursday to say they were coming | The crow cawed louder than it usually does | Crows can sense visitors from far away | She guessed because guests often come on Sundays
exercise: Mason's mum says he is better at maths because the mirror's seven bad years are over. What is the real reason? | He has practised his times tables every night | The broken mirror's bad luck ran out | Ten is a lucky age for maths | His mum has stopped worrying about mirrors, so he feels much calmer
exercise: What do grown-ups call remembering the times that fit your idea and forgetting the rest? | Confirmation bias | Moving the goalposts | Having a good memory | A fair test
exercise: In Britain and Japan a black cat is often called good luck. What does that show? | The "rule" is made up, since places disagree | Black cats really are luckier in some countries | Cats behave much better in those two countries | Bad luck only works in some countries and not others
test: Grandson: "Grandad, the players can't even see your socks, mate. You're not on the team sheet." What is he making fun of? | The idea that socks at home can change the score | Grandad's taste in socks | Grandad being old | The colour red
test: Nan: "Aye, and I suppose my slippers won the cup in '86." What does she mean? | By the same logic any old thing could win a match | She really believes her slippers helped her team win the cup back then | Slippers are luckier than socks | She played in the cup final in 1986
test: Big sister, fed up: "Shut it about the cat, Nan, it's three streets away having a nap." Which is true? | The cat had nothing to do with the dropped jar | The cat made Nan so nervous that she dropped it | Black cats only bring bad luck on Fridays, not today | Jars break much more often in the hour after lunch
test: What would happen if Grandad wrote down every match for a whole season, with socks and without? | He would find the socks make no difference | He would find the team only wins with the socks | He would find the team only loses with the socks | He would find the socks only work away from home
test: In a cartoon a man walks under a ladder and a piano falls on him "because of bad luck". Put it right. | Things can fall from above a ladder; it's about safety, not luck | Ladders really do bring bad luck, so pianos fall | Pianos only fall near ladders on Fridays | Walking under ladders is always safe, even while people are working up on them
test: Sofia: "So if I'm unlucky it's the broom, and if I'm lucky it's also the broom?" What trap has she spotted? | A rule that fits every result and so explains nothing | Finding money on the floor is always a sign of good luck | Sweeping at night is actually lucky after all, not unlucky | Brooms work quite differently in the daytime than at night
test: Ellie says, "Absolute weapon of a plan, Dad," about skipping floor 13. What does she mean? | It's a silly plan, because a new number doesn't change the floor | It's a brilliant plan and she is impressed | The plan is dangerous, like a weapon | She wants to sleep on floor 12 instead
test: Noah: "Ammamma, the crow's not on WhatsApp." Why is this a good comeback? | It points to the real cause, the phone call | It shows that crows can't count guests | It means crows only use other apps | It proves that guests never come on Sundays
test: Which is a real reason to be careful opening an umbrella indoors? | You could knock a lamp or poke someone's eye | It lets bad luck into the house | It makes pets ill the next week | It breaks the umbrella's good-luck charm, so it can't protect you
test: Your mate: "Wore my lucky pants and got 10 out of 10, no cap" ("no cap" means honestly). What is the best reply? | "Did you revise too? Try without them and count." | "Lucky pants only work on Fridays, mate, so it's a fluke." | "Wear your lucky socks as well next time, for double luck." | "That's proof, bro, wear them for every single test now."
test: Callum's sister: "Aye, the shoes sat the test for him." What is she doing? | Being sarcastic about Gran's reasoning | Saying shoes really can take tests | Calling her brother stupid | Praising Gran's new shoes
exam: Identify the principal error in the inference that a black cat crossing the road caused a jar to be dropped later the same day. | The assumption that an earlier event necessarily caused a later one | The failure to ascertain the precise colour of the cat | The omission of the day of the week from the account | The supposition that glass jars are fragile
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a hotel whose lift omits the numeral thirteen. | The floor designated fourteen is in fact the thirteenth storey | The floor designated fourteen is the fourteenth storey | The thirteenth storey has been removed entirely from the structure of the building | The floor designated twelve is the thirteenth storey
exam: Determine which procedure would constitute a fair test of whether a charm confers good fortune. | A count of outcomes with and without the charm over many occasions | A single trial conducted in the presence of the charm | The sworn testimony of the owner of the charm | An enquiry into the age and origin of the charm
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises a belief that is maintained by supplying a fresh excuse upon each failure? | It cannot be shown to be wrong and is therefore uninformative | It is confirmed afresh by each failure | It is proven by the number of excuses offered | It becomes steadily more probable with the passage of time and further failures
